1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,066
Lesson 3.7.

2
00:00:02,446 --> 00:00:05,514
Thermodynamics of resetting
and error-free readout.

3
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:09,965
Every computer repeats a
cycle: it puts its memory in

4
00:00:09,965 --> 00:00:12,871
a known state, computes,
and reads the result.

5
00:00:13,251 --> 00:00:18,815
In lesson 1.5 we saw the cost of
erasing a completely unknown bit.

6
00:00:19,195 --> 00:00:22,677
But in practice memory
is almost never uniform,

7
00:00:22,677 --> 00:00:26,308
reset is never perfect,
and readout is noisy too.

8
00:00:26,688 --> 00:00:29,770
This lesson puts a price
on all three: resetting,

9
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:32,476
retaining and reading
a bit without errors.

10
00:00:33,583 --> 00:00:36,755
Suppose the memory is
one with probability p

11
00:00:36,755 --> 00:00:39,494
and zero with probability one minus p.

12
00:00:39,874 --> 00:00:45,710
Resetting it to zero removes its
Shannon entropy, so the generalized

13
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:51,202
Landauer bound is W ≥ k_B T H(p),
with entropy measured in nats.

14
00:00:51,582 --> 00:00:56,208
The curve shows that bound: it
equals k_B T ln 2 when the bit is

15
00:00:56,208 --> 00:01:00,328
uniform and falls to zero when
we already know its value.

16
00:01:00,708 --> 00:01:04,274
Resetting a bit that is almost
always zero is almost free.

17
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:09,065
No real reset is perfect:
at the end there remains an

18
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:12,261
error probability ε that
the bit is still one.

19
00:01:12,641 --> 00:01:17,452
The final memory still
has entropy H(ε), and

20
00:01:17,452 --> 00:01:22,043
the bound becomes W ≥ k_B T (H(p) − H(ε)).

21
00:01:22,423 --> 00:01:26,148
With a uniform bit and
a one-percent error,

22
00:01:26,148 --> 00:01:29,786
the minimum drops from 0.69 to 0.64 k_B T.

23
00:01:30,166 --> 00:01:33,579
Accepting errors makes reset
cheaper, but only slightly.

24
00:01:33,959 --> 00:01:36,653
And if the protocol
takes a finite time, the

25
00:01:36,653 --> 00:01:39,224
excess that decays as one over τ is added.

26
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:43,143
A reset bit must also stay put.

27
00:01:43,523 --> 00:01:47,766
In a double well, thermal
noise makes it hop the

28
00:01:47,766 --> 00:01:52,097
barrier at the Arrhenius
rate, ν₀ e^(−E_b/k_B T).

29
00:01:52,477 --> 00:01:58,271
For the probability of a hop
within a time t to be below ε,

30
00:01:58,271 --> 00:02:03,181
the barrier must satisfy
E_b ≥ k_B T ln(ν₀ t / ε).

31
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:07,667
With an attempt frequency of
10¹² Hz and an error of one in

32
00:02:07,667 --> 00:02:11,355
a billion, keeping the bit
for one second takes about

33
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:14,973
forty-eight k_B T; ten years
take about sixty-eight.

34
00:02:15,353 --> 00:02:18,488
Retention grows exponentially
with the barrier, but

35
00:02:18,488 --> 00:02:21,438
the barrier only grows
with the log of the time.

36
00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:23,708
Now let's read the bit.

37
00:02:24,088 --> 00:02:27,306
The sensor is a node
of capacitance C whose

38
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:30,748
voltage has thermal noise
of variance k_B T/C.

39
00:02:31,128 --> 00:02:33,548
A comparator decides
zero or one depending on

40
00:02:33,548 --> 00:02:36,130
whether the voltage exceeds
a threshold halfway.

41
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:41,021
The error probability is
the Gaussian tail, Q(V/σ).

42
00:02:41,401 --> 00:02:46,512
For an error of one in a billion,
the signal must be six standard

43
00:02:46,512 --> 00:02:52,253
deviations, and the signal energy, ½
C V², comes to about eighteen k_B T.

44
00:02:52,633 --> 00:02:57,513
In general that energy grows
as k_B T ln(1/ε): each factor

45
00:02:57,513 --> 00:03:01,888
of ten in reliability costs
a little over two k_B T.

46
00:03:03,017 --> 00:03:05,169
Where is the unavoidable cost of reading?

47
00:03:05,549 --> 00:03:08,794
As with Maxwell's demon,
copying the bit into

48
00:03:08,794 --> 00:03:11,535
a clean memory can be done reversibly.

49
00:03:11,915 --> 00:03:15,976
What cannot be avoided is resetting
that memory for the next readout.

50
00:03:16,356 --> 00:03:20,049
If the result is discarded,
each readout pays at

51
00:03:20,049 --> 00:03:23,588
least the result's
entropy, k_B T H, at reset.

52
00:03:23,968 --> 00:03:28,233
That is why a chip that reads pbits
millions of times per second spends

53
00:03:28,233 --> 00:03:32,619
readout and reset energy that can far
exceed that of the sampling itself.

54
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:36,282
Let's gather the numbers
at three hundred kelvin.

55
00:03:36,662 --> 00:03:41,716
Resetting a uniform bit
with a one-percent error

56
00:03:41,716 --> 00:03:46,664
costs at least 0.64 k_B
T, about 2.6 × 10⁻²¹ J.

57
00:03:47,044 --> 00:03:51,494
Reading it with an error of
one in a billion requires

58
00:03:51,494 --> 00:03:55,357
an eighteen k_B T signal,
about 7.5 × 10⁻²⁰ J.

59
00:03:55,737 --> 00:03:59,734
And keeping it for ten years requires
a barrier of sixty-eight k_B T.

60
00:04:00,114 --> 00:04:04,170
Reliable readout costs almost thirty
times more than the ideal reset.

61
00:04:05,283 --> 00:04:07,784
In hardware these
accounts drive the design.

62
00:04:08,164 --> 00:04:12,003
In a thermodynamic chip, the
input and output energy to read

63
00:04:12,003 --> 00:04:15,522
pbits often dominates over
that of the sampling fabric.

64
00:04:15,902 --> 00:04:19,231
Reading less often, spacing
readouts more than the

65
00:04:19,231 --> 00:04:23,358
autocorrelation time, saves
energy without losing information.

66
00:04:23,738 --> 00:04:28,284
In Torx, each Gibbs update is a
reset followed by a gate, and we will

67
00:04:28,284 --> 00:04:32,171
see the difference between an
ideal reset and a finite one.

68
00:04:33,283 --> 00:04:37,158
Frequent mistakes: using
k_B T ln 2 for any reset,

69
00:04:37,158 --> 00:04:40,955
when the bound depends on
the prior distribution.

70
00:04:41,335 --> 00:04:45,123
Confusing the barrier that retains
the bit with the cost of resetting it.

71
00:04:45,503 --> 00:04:48,897
Believing reading is free because
the copy can be reversible.

72
00:04:49,277 --> 00:04:50,848
And mixing bits with nats.

73
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:56,624
In summary: resetting costs at
least k_B T (H(p) − H(ε)), which

74
00:04:56,624 --> 00:05:00,927
depends on what we already
know and on the accepted error.

75
00:05:01,307 --> 00:05:03,632
Retaining a bit requires a barrier that

76
00:05:03,632 --> 00:05:06,076
grows with the log of the retention time.

77
00:05:06,456 --> 00:05:12,348
Reading with error ε requires a
signal of about k_B T ln(1/ε).

78
00:05:12,728 --> 00:05:15,253
And the unavoidable cost
of reading appears when

79
00:05:15,253 --> 00:05:17,830
resetting the memory where
the result was stored.

80
00:05:18,950 --> 00:05:23,601
To practice: compute the cost of
resetting a biased bit with a given error.

81
00:05:23,981 --> 00:05:26,978
Find the barrier to
retain a bit for one hour.

82
00:05:27,358 --> 00:05:30,572
Compute the energy and
voltage of a reliable readout.

83
00:05:30,952 --> 00:05:36,474
And explain why reading every sweep
wastes energy when samples are correlated.
